---
import UtilityLayout from "../layouts/UtilityLayout.astro";
---

<UtilityLayout
  title="Privacy & operating limits"
  description="What UtilityPot sends to Jev, what stays in your browser, and how public-page fetching and inference are bounded."
  slug=""
  canonicalPath="/privacy/"
>
  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Utilities that stay in your browser</h2>
    <p>
      CSV/JSON conversion, date cleanup, cron previews and meeting-time
      calculation run in your browser without AI. Diagram rendering is also
      local after its source steps and connections have been interpreted by AI.
      This application has no account database, saved input history, session
      replay or input analytics. Anonymous, opt-out-respecting PostHog analytics
      measure page views and coarse tool actions, using a pseudonymous browser
      identifier in local storage rather than analytics cookies. SDK opt-out is
      stored in the browser. Pageview URLs retain the public path but remove
      query strings and fragments; referrers are reduced to their origin, and
      campaign/ad-click identifiers are discarded, including persisted
      acquisition properties. Analytics does not record form values, page text,
      provider responses, or output. Family and creator discovery clicks record
      only a fixed destination name and placement (intro, result or project
      showcase), not link text or input. These clicks do not establish return
      visits. Local computation and downloads remain in your browser; download
      events measure user intent, not completed transfer. Hosting providers
      still process ordinary request metadata under their own policies.
    </p>
    <h2>Image redaction and spreadsheet comparisons</h2>
    <p>
      Screenshot redaction and spreadsheet comparison run on your device,
      without uploading images or spreadsheet contents. Redaction is manual:
      only selected regions become opaque black pixels in a newly encoded PNG.
      Review the whole downloaded image before sharing; unselected sensitive
      content remains visible. The original image stays separate and must be
      kept private.
    </p>
    <p>
      Spreadsheet comparisons match exact values by a chosen unique key. XLSX
      formulas are compared as text, never executed, and visual formatting is
      not compared. CSV reports protect potentially executable spreadsheet
      values by prefixing an apostrophe; JSON preserves literal report values.
      Each tool displays its file and resource limits. Clearing or replacing
      sources discards the active result; these tools do not save an input
      history.
    </p>
    <h2>Screenshot recognition</h2>
    <p>
      Screenshot-to-spreadsheet runs OCR and table reconstruction on your
      device. Recognition scripts and English language data download from this
      site when you extract a table; your browser may cache the language data
      for later use. Images, recognized text and edited cells are not uploaded
      or saved as an input history. Clear or replace the image to discard the
      active result. Supported images are PNG, JPEG and WebP, up to 10 MB and
      eight million decoded pixels. English printed tables work best; merged
      cells, handwriting, rotated photographs and unclear text may be misread.
      Review every value. XLSX exports store cells as text. Clipboard/CSV
      formula protection is on by default and prefixes potentially executable
      spreadsheet values with an apostrophe; disabling it may let spreadsheet
      applications execute formulas.
    </p>
    <h2>Utilities that use AI</h2>
    <p>
      Entity extraction, flowchart interpretation and webpage evidence selection
      use AI. After explicit consent, the context described beside the primary
      action is sent to our Cloudflare Worker and TypeSafe. This can include
      source text, category definitions, step relationships or questions. Do not
      submit confidential or personal information without authority. The
      application does not persist these inputs or log provider bodies. TypeSafe
      processing and retention are governed by its terms, not a claim that
      inference is fully local.
    </p>
    <h2>Public-page reading</h2>
    <p>
      Reading a public page sends its URL to our server. Cloudflare resolves the
      public hostname and fetches HTTP/HTTPS pages through its public Internet
      network boundary; URL and DNS checks repeat on every redirect. The
      destination receives the requested URL, and Cloudflare DNS receives the
      hostname. No browser cookies, authorization or provider secrets are
      forwarded. The reader executes no scripts or embedded requests, accepts
      only UTF-8 HTML/plain text, and caps reading at 250,000 bytes, eight
      seconds and three redirects. Only 10,000 characters / 100 paragraphs are
      searched, with truncation disclosed. Blocked, authenticated,
      JavaScript-only or unsupported pages can be used by pasting text. This is
      not a paywall bypass.
    </p>
    <h2>Secrets and spending</h2>
    <p>
      TYPESAFE_API_KEY is a server-side Worker secret. The browser never
      receives it. Requests are limited to 16 KB and a bounded set of
      server-authored questions. Each provider request has a 15-second timeout;
      an entity section has a 30-second total deadline. Documents up to 2 MiB
      run in complete-source sections, paced to respect the existing request
      limits. A failed section rejects the whole result; there are no automatic
      retries. Client and aggregate limits are 6 and 30 requests per minute per
      Cloudflare location. These limits are eventually consistent and are not a
      strict global billing cap. Provider-side spend limits are required before
      public AI launch.
    </p>
    <h2>Output boundaries</h2>
    <p>
      Typed choices and confidence do not guarantee correctness. Review results.
      Entity extraction can miss or mislabel mentions despite processing every
      section. Source excerpts are not independent fact verification. Missing
      credentials, protection or valid output produce an explicit failure, not
      fake model results.
    </p>
  </section>
</UtilityLayout>
